{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ea7174ed-1558-4981-a3cb-2f12db46f50a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d968c42686c00f1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-fwig8g3z/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic runs a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerator hardware to establish functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the platform's supported model set, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models are productized for ROCm serving. It resolves the central unknown of the AMD enablement strategy: whether upstream vLLM ROCm builds deliver acceptable functional correctness and performance for the models the platform supports, measured first-hand rather than assumed from upstream claims.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the validation matrix: the supported model set to be tested, functional pass/fail criteria per model (correct outputs against the CUDA baseline behavior), and performance parity comparison targets versus the productized CUDA builds.\n- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or partner-lab access — see Risks); stand up the test environment with the required ROCm driver level.\n- Execute the functional validation runs of vLLM ROCm serving builds for each model in the matrix on the physical hardware.\n- Execute the performance benchmark runs and collect parity data (throughput, latency) against the CUDA baseline for the same models and serving configurations.\n- Produce the validation report: per-model functional results, performance parity data, and the go/no-go recommendation with the cleared model list for productization.\n- Produce the go/no-go scope document that downstream productization work builds against.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The validation matrix (model set, functional pass/fail criteria, performance parity baselines) is documented and reviewed before campaign execution begins.\n- Functional validation results exist for every model in the validated matrix, each recorded as pass or fail against the defined criteria.\n- Performance parity data is collected for the models that pass functional validation, with the serving configuration and hardware specification recorded alongside the measurements.\n- The validation report states, for each model in the matrix, whether it is cleared for productization, and the report is delivered.\n- The go/no-go scope for productization is published, enumerating the cleared model list (which may be empty or partial) with the supporting data.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Hardware access is an external availability precondition: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet, and procurement or partner-lab access is unresolved (strategy open question 1). The epic's work includes securing access, but the grant itself is outside the strategy's control and may delay campaign start; the acceptance criteria sit on the campaign's deliverables, not on the access grant.\n- Upstream ROCm image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; the campaign tests against the image versions available at execution time.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The campaign outcome gates the productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime: a partial clear rewrites the productization scope to the cleared model list; a full no-go removes the productization work entirely.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":30639,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae4