{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b84d6983c07ff0e0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 (Implementation)\n2\t\n3\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n4\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n5\t| change_specificity | +1 | Scope is specific: build a ROCm variant of the vLLM runtime image in the existing midstream repo (opendatahub-io/vllm), define the ServingRuntime configuration, and release through the standard serving runtimes pipeline — mirroring the known CUDA productization recipe. The only variable input (cleared model list) arrives as the gating investigation's deliverable. |\n6\t| pattern_precedent | +1 | The same midstream repo already productizes CUDA vLLM runtime builds per release; a new accelerator image variant follows the established build/release/ServingRuntime pattern. |\n7\t| adapter_pattern | 0 | No plugin/adapter reference implementation for a ROCm variant exists in our midstream codebase; upstream publishes ROCm builds but this is not an in-repo adapter pattern. |\n8\t| existing_foundation | +1 | Extends the existing productized vLLM runtime and serving runtime support; no greenfield surface. |\n9\t| open_questions | 0 | The scope-defining unknown (which models clear) is resolved by the gating investigation and captured as a gate, not as an unresolved design decision. Remaining choices (pinned ROCm base image version, release-train alignment) are implementation details resolved at execution time. |\n10\t| external_dependency | -1 | Upstream ROCm runtime image release cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities (strategy open question 2); version pinning and release-train alignment depend on their publishing schedule, though no code contribution is needed. |\n11\t| human_process_gates | 0 | No approval step beyond the normal build/release and PR review process. |\n12\t| repo_access | +1 | The midstream repo (opendatahub-io/vllm) is accessible and modifia