ity collapse: each epic maps to exactly one HLR, so no deferrable features are bundled with P0 work.\n55\t\n56\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n57\t\n58\tDiagram edges (E001→E002, E001→E004, E002→E004, E003→E004) match the frontmatter `dependencies` lists exactly, in the correct arrow direction (dependency → dependent). No cycles, no invalid references. Critical path length 3 (E001→E002→E004) is appropriate for a medium strategy. Rule 11 is satisfied: the docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic (E002, E003). The E001→E004 edge is a direct artifact-consumption edge (docs consume the validation report and cleared model list) and serializes nothing beyond the existing transitive path E001→E002→E004. Naturally parallel work is not serialized: E001∥E003 and E002∥E003 have no edges, correctly justified by Rule 14 (different components/repos, no shared artifacts). No `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics exist (all components are Konflux-onboarded per their architecture-context files; the ROCm vLLm build is a new image variant in the existing midstream repo), so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism checks do not apply. No conditional branch files exist, so the branch-file rules do not apply. Cross-checking epic content found no data/artifact dependency missing from the DAG.\n59\t\n60\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n61\t\n62\tComponent/team tuples are clean and all component values (Serving Runtimes, Distributed Workloads, Documentation) appear verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. No epic bundles multiple teams. The KServe and Training Operator spans are correctly handled via the active/passive rule (same owning team as the active work, validation folded into E002/E003 acceptance criteria, documented in the summary's Component Mapping Notes). No scope duplication across siblings — each epic builds a distinct surface. No work-product type mixing: E004 is pure content authoring. One watch item: E001 plausibly exceeds the ~2-week guideline (matrix definition, hardware/environment bring-up, functional plus performance runs across the supported model set, report) as one logical change where a split would fragment the campaign — recorded as a minor issue with a concrete re-split trigger, so the overage is a managed trade-off and this criterion keeps its score.\n63\t\n64\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n65\t\n66\tEpic types are correct: E001 is a genuine Investigation (its go/no-go outcome rewrites or removes E002's scope), and E002/E003/E004 produce concrete artifacts. E002's gate metadata is correct (`gated_by: E001`, `gate_failure_impact` with `action: rewrite` and a populated `fallback_approach`, target present in direct `dependencies`). However, **E004 lists Investigation E001 in its `dependencies` with `gated_by: null`** — a major issue. Per Rule 3, every downstream epic gated by an Investigation is a true gate recorded in frontmatter, and the epic-template rule states every epic that depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to it. The campaign outcome genuinely rewrites E004's scope: its validated-model-list topic is exactly the cleared list (E004's own AC: \"The documented model list matches exactly the models cleared for productization by the validation campaign\"), a partial clear narrows it, and a full no-go removes the serving-docs scope — an outcome E002's `gate_failure_impact` does not propagate to E004. Correction: set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` on E004 with `gate_failure_impact` (action `rewrite`; fallback describing the partial-clear and full-no-go documentation handling). Major issue → this criterion scores 0 → auto-fail.\n67\t\n68\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n69\t\n70\tSignal fields are type-correct (E001 carries `investigation_signals`; E002/E003/E004 carry `ai_signals`; ai-signals rationale files exist for all four epics and match the frontmatter values). E001's investigation signals are well-calibrated against the rubric (question_specificity +1 for enumerable per-model pass/fail; source_accessibility 0 since no readable artifact holds the answer; cluster_hardware_dependence -2 since the gating question needs live AMD GPUs; human_judgment_required -1 for peripheral procurement coordination). `architecture_claims: +1` values are corroborated and cite real context files (serving-runtimes.md confirms only CUDA builds are productized with ROCm upstream-only; distributed-workloads.md and training-operator.md confirm the Kueue quota/gang-admission and PyTorchJob surfaces). One arguable value: E003's `external_dependency: 0` with the rationale \"no upstream PR/RFC or vendor coordination required for this epic's deliverable\" conflicts with the epic's own Risks section — its validation ACs (single-/multi-GPU PyTorchJobs running to completion on AMD GPU nodes) require AMD Instinct access whose procurement/partner-lab grant is unresolved (strategy open question 1), and that coordination is captured in E001's signals but in none of E003's. Minor issue; 1 arguable value → score 1.\n71\t\n72\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n73\t\n74\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E004 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review sign-off AC; E002 has the \"pipeline green for the release build\" AC; no epic is a capability replacement, so rollback/feature-flag ACs are not required (the ROCm variant is additive alongside CUDA). The hardware-access precondition is correctly kept out of the ACs — it sits in E001's Risks with an explicit statement that the ACs sit on the campaign's deliverables, not the access grant; this is exactly the soft-AC trap avoided. No AC states an externally-controlled precondition. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (E004's Notes reference them by name).\n75\t\n76\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n77\t\n78\tAll strategy scope is covered: serving validation (E001), runtime productization with KServe passive validation (E002), training support with Training Operator passive validation and amd.com/gpu quota (E003), and documentation (E004). The out-of-scope boundaries (other accelerator families, AMD GPU sharing/partitioning) are carried forward as an explicit \"Limitations\" docs topic in E004. Strategy open questions are carried into Health Warnings and epic Risks — open question 2 (upstream image cadence) on E002, open question 1 (hardware access) on E001 and E003. The strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, but HLR-4 explicitly requires documentation and a docs-authoring epic exists. The go/no-go outcome handling (full/partial/no-go) is captured via E002's rewrite gate with an explicit fallback, an acceptable alternative to conditional branch files given the variable partial-clear model set. One minor nit: open question 1 is not carried into E002's Risks even though its KServe validation AC requires serving on AMD GPU nodes — the same external dependency E003 acknowledges.\n79\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8514bdcd6f1b742","agent_id":"a10a83694d4bb48b5","session_id":"fe501be6-c4a7-499a-b593-08b3eb0d5405","uuid":"a25f0f90-55e9-497f-82a5-63b7dc699f0b","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:20:19.959Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1333e1f2cae354e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
