{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3ee48f9d71f8267","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n11\t\n12\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training.\n13\t\n14\t## Epic List\n15\t\n16\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n17\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support with Kueue admission and amd.com/gpu quota | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Document AMD hardware support, driver prerequisites, and validated model list | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n22\t\n23\t## Dependency DAG\n24\t\n25\t```mermaid\n26\tgraph TD\n27\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign]\n28\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 Productize ROCm vLLM runtime]\n29\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 ROCm training support]\n30\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Documentation]\n31\t    E001 --> E002\n32\t    E001 --> E004\n33\t    E002 --> E004\n34\t    E003 --> E004\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\t## DAG Justification\n38\t\n39\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n40\t|------|------|-----------|\n41\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's outcome (which models clear functional/performance parity, go/no-go) determines the scope of the productization epic. True gate: `rewrite` — the model set and go/no-go decision rewrite the productization scope; a full no-go removes the epic. Recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n42\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) + Rule 12 analog (artifact consumer) | The documentation's validated model list and hardware data are the campaign's deliverable; docs cannot be authored before the data exists. True gate: `rewrite` — the campaign outcome rewrites the docs scope. A partial clear narrows the documented model list to the cleared models; a full no-go removes the serving-docs topics (leaving the parity findings and the training topics). Recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E004. |\n43\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes the productized runtime. |\n44\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation covers training support (driver prerequisites, amd.com/gpu quota usage). |\n45\t| No edge E001 ↔ E003 | Rule 14 (independent, different repos/components) | Training support is validated independently of the serving parity campaign; no shared artifact. |\n46\t| No edge E003 → E002 | Rule 14 | Serving productization and training support are in different components/repos with no shared artifacts. |\n47\t\n48\t## Component Mapping Notes\n49\t\n50\t- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n51\t- **KServe** (strategy Affected Components): passive component — no code changes indicated; KServe deploys ServingRuntimes via its existing CRD flow and ROCm is a runtime-image concern. Same owning team (RHOAI Serving) as the active Serving Runtimes work, so per the active/passive rule its validation is an acceptance criterion on E002 (InferenceService serving through the standard KServe flow on AMD GPUs) rather than a separate epic.\n52\t- **Training Operator** (strategy Affected Components): passive for HLR-3 — PyTorchJob CRDs admit arbitrary resource names and container images, so no Training Operator code change is indicated. Same owning team (Distributed Workloads) as the active Distributed Workloads work, so its validation is acceptance criteria on E003. E003's `component` is `Distributed Workloads` (the quota/admission changes land there); the span over Training Operator validation is noted here.\n53\t- **E004 team**: assigned to RHOAI Serving because the validated model list and runtime/driver prerequisites are serving-centric; the training portion is covered within the same docs topic set.\n54\t- **No onboarding chain**: all active components exist in `.context/architecture-context/` and are Konflux-onboarded per their context files; the ROCm vLLM build is a new image variant in the existing midstream repo (opendatahub-io/vllm), so no `repo-onboarding` or `konflux-onboarding` epic is generated (Rule 20, existing-repo image case).\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n57\t\n58\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n59\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n60\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; produces parity data and go/no-go scope. |\n61\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productization of the ROCm vLLM serving runtime for cleared models; gated by the campaign outcome. Includes KServe passive validation. |\n62\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training with Kueue admission and amd.com/gpu quota; includes Training Operator passive validation. |\n63\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Documentation epic. Epic priority is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), exempt from inheriting HLR-4's P2 label and from pushing priority upstream. |\n64\t\n65\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n66\t\n67\t## Health Warnings\n68\t\n69\t- **Hardware access is an external availability precondition (open question 1)**: the validation campaign (E001) requires dedicated AMD Instinct nodes and none exist in the CI fleet; procurement or partner-lab access is unresolved. This is not an acceptance criterion on E001 (the epic does not control the grant); it is recorded as a risk on E001. Campaign start date is exposed to external scheduling the strategy cannot compress.\n70\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence is externally controlled (open question 2)**: whether AMD's runtime image releases align with our release trains affects E002's version pinning and release timing, not the existence or structure of any epic. Handled as an acceptance criterion (pinned image version and release-train alignment documented) and risk on E002 rather than a DAG edge or separate epic.\n71\t- **E001 plausibly exceeds the ~2-week epic guideline (managed trade-off)**: the validation campaign bundles matrix definition, hardware/environment bring-up, functional plus performance runs across the supported model set, and the report into one logical change — splitting it now would fragment the campaign and duplicate the environment bring-up across pieces. Re-split trigger: if environment/hardware bring-up or the model matrix outgrows a sprint (e.g. the supported model set expands materially, or environment bring-up and validation execution cannot share one sprint's capacity), split the environment/hardware bring-up work (securing AMD Instinct access, standing up the test environment with the required ROCm driver level) from the validation execution and reporting.\n72\t- No priority inversions detected: P0 serving path (E001 → E002) matches the strategic customer driver; training (P1) and docs follow.\n73\t\n74\t## Tiered Delivery\n75\t\n76\tThe upstream ROCm image cadence is an external dependency with uncertain timing (Rule 18 / Step 5.5):\n77\t\n78\t- **Tier 1 (independent partial value)**: E001 (parity data and go/no-go scope — risk-reduction value on its own) and E003 (training support on upstream PyTorch ROCm images, independent of serving release alignment). These run in parallel and deliver value regardless of upstream image cadence.\n79\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E002 (productized runtime — gated by the campaign outcome and by pinning an upstream ROCm image that aligns with a release train) and E004 (docs, last per Rule 11).\n80\t\n81\tThe existing DAG already expresses this tiering (E001 → E002 → E004); no additional structural split is required.\n82\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2af2e29c81a4d5d","agent_id":"a403baa3d0b94eac1","session_id":"fe501be6-c4a7-499a-b593-08b3eb0d5405","uuid":"02c10831-988b-49ea-81bc-aad3c0db235e","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:22:37.812Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3ee48f9d71f8267","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
