{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d46e8437a5ae53d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 0\n12\t  local_runnability: 0\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n14\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis epic runs a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerator hardware to establish functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the platform's supported model set, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models are productized for ROCm serving. It resolves the central unknown of the AMD enablement strategy: whether upstream vLLM ROCm builds deliver acceptable functional correctness and performance for the models the platform supports, measured first-hand rather than assumed from upstream claims.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Define the validation matrix: the supported model set to be tested, functional pass/fail criteria per model (correct outputs against the CUDA baseline behavior), and performance parity comparison targets versus the productized CUDA builds.\n34\t- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or partner-lab access — see Risks); stand up the test environment with the required ROCm driver level.\n35\t- Execute the functional validation runs of vLLM ROCm serving builds for each model in the matrix on the physical hardware.\n36\t- Execute the performance benchmark runs and collect parity data (throughput, latency) against the CUDA baseline for the same models and serving configurations.\n37\t- Produce the validation report: per-model functional results, performance parity data, and the go/no-go recommendation with the cleared model list for productization.\n38\t- Produce the go/no-go scope document that downstream productization work builds against.\n39\t\n40\t## Acceptance Criteria\n41\t\n42\t- The validation matrix (model set, functional pass/fail criteria, performance parity baselines) is documented and reviewed before campaign execution begins.\n43\t- Functional validation results exist for every model in the validated matrix, each recorded as pass or fail against the defined criteria.\n44\t- Performance parity data is collected for the models that pass functional validation, with the serving configuration and hardware specification recorded alongside the measurements.\n45\t- The validation report states, for each model in the matrix, whether it is cleared for productization, and the report is delivered.\n46\t- The go/no-go scope for productization is published, enumerating the cleared model list (which may be empty or partial) with the supporting data.\n47\t\n48\t## HLR Traceability\n49\t\n50\t- HLR-1 (P0): A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n51\t\n52\t## Risks\n53\t\n54\t- Hardware access is an external availability precondition: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet, and procurement or partner-lab access is unresolved (strategy open question 1). The epic's work includes securing access, but the grant itself is outside the strategy's control and may delay campaign start; the acceptance criteria sit on the campaign's deliverables, not on the access grant.\n55\t- Upstream ROCm image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; the campaign tests against the image versions available at execution time.\n56\t\n57\t## Notes\n58\t\n59\t- The campaign outcome gates the productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime: a partial clear rewrites the productization scope to the cleared model list; a full no-go removes the productization work entirely.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2af2e29c81a4d5d","agent_id":"a403baa3d0b94eac1","session_id":"fe501be6-c4a7-499a-b593-08b3eb0d5405","uuid":"f7492abe-3da0-4991-a888-f51def1e7356","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:22:42.379Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d46e8437a5ae53d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
